  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+3X+2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+3X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(H)={H}^{2}+3H+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${H}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{2} & =H\\cdot H \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(H):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(H) & ={H}^{2}+3H+2I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7+9+2 & -3+(-3)+0 \\\\ 6+6+0 & -2+0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -6 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
